What machine the program runs on.

| Function | Returns |
|---|---|
| `sys::processor_count()` | processors online now, at least 1 |
| `sys::gethostname(buffer)` | the host name written into buffer, NUL-terminated, and its length |
| `sys::uname()` | a `system_name`: `system()`, `node()`, `release()`, `version()`, `machine()` |

```c++
#include "ca/machine.hpp"
#include "ca/sv.hpp"

auto main(void) -> int {
	const ca::usz workers = ca::sys::processor_count();
	if (workers < 1U)
		return 1;

	auto names = ca::sys::uname();
	if (!names || names->system().empty() || names->machine().empty())
		return 2;

	char host[256]{};
	auto length = ca::sys::gethostname(host);
	if (!length || ca::sv::str{host, *length} != names->node())
		return 3;

	char tiny[1]{};
	return ca::sys::gethostname(tiny) ? 4 : 0;
}
```

The views of `system_name` point into the object, which holds the names inline: they live as
long as it does. `gethostname` fails when the name does not fit, instead of truncating it.
